---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
const checker =
  import.meta.env.PUBLIC_CHECKER_ORIGIN || 'http://localhost:5173';
---

<Page
  title="A clearer view of who follows you back"
  description="MutualLens is a follow-back checker in preview. Understand followers and following with readable reports, explicit source completeness, and local import tools."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">YOUR CONNECTIONS, WITH CONTEXT</p><h1>
        A clearer view of<br />who follows you back.
      </h1><p class="hero-description">
        Less guesswork. More perspective. MutualLens puts followers and
        following side by side, so you can understand the overlap.
      </p><div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button primary" href={checker}
          >Check automatically <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
        ><a class="secondary-link" href={`${checker}/#import`}
          >Import your Instagram export</a
        >
      </div><p class="availability">
        <span aria-hidden="true">○</span> Automatic checking is unavailable in this
        preview.
      </p><a class="sample-link" href={checker}
        >Explore clearly labeled synthetic sample data <span aria-hidden="true"
          >→</span
        ></a
      >
    </div>
    <div
      class="report-preview"
      aria-label="Illustrative synthetic report, not live Instagram data"
    >
      <div class="preview-top">
        <span>YOUR CONNECTIONS</span><span class="mini-label"
          >Synthetic example</span
        >
      </div><div class="preview-report-title">The overlap, made clear.</div><div
        class="preview-metrics"
      >
        <div><strong>6,000</strong><span>Followers</span></div><div>
          <strong>6,000</strong><span>Following</span>
        </div><div><strong>4,500</strong><span>Mutuals</span></div>
      </div><div class="preview-category">
        <span>Not following you back</span><span>1,500</span>
      </div><div class="preview-rows">
        {
          ['user06001', 'user06002', 'user06003', 'user06004'].map(
            (username) => (
              <div>
                <>
                  <span class="account-marker" aria-hidden="true">
                    U
                  </span>
                  <strong>@{username}</strong>
                  <span>Synthetic</span>
                </>
              </div>
            ),
          )
        }
      </div><p class="preview-caption">
        An illustration using fictional records. No account was checked.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <div class="principles">
    <span><span aria-hidden="true">01</span> Readable lists</span><span
      ><span aria-hidden="true">02</span> Context with every result</span
    ><span><span aria-hidden="true">03</span> An ad-free checker</span>
  </div>
  <section class="section-block explanation">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">TWO LISTS. A BETTER PICTURE.</p><h2>
        Make sense of<br />your connections.
      </h2><p>
        Followers and following describe different directions. A follow-back
        report compares them, without making assumptions about anyone’s reasons.
      </p><a class="text-link" href="/guides/followers-vs-following/"
        >Understand the three groups <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a
      >
    </div><div class="definition-list">
      <article>
        <span class="definition-number">01</span><div>
          <h3>Mutuals</h3><p>
            You follow them, and they appear in your followers list.
          </p>
        </div><span class="relation-symbol" aria-hidden="true">↔</span>
      </article><article>
        <span class="definition-number">02</span><div>
          <h3>Not following you back</h3><p>
            You follow them, but they are absent from a complete supplied
            followers list.
          </p>
        </div><span class="relation-symbol" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </article><article>
        <span class="definition-number">03</span><div>
          <h3>You don’t follow back</h3><p>
            They follow you, but are absent from your complete supplied
            following list.
          </p>
        </div><span class="relation-symbol" aria-hidden="true">←</span>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="integrity-panel">
    <div class="eyebrow">A NOTE ON AVAILABILITY</div><h2>
      Useful results need complete sources.
    </h2><p>
      MutualLens is still a preview. Website-only automatic acquisition has not
      passed its live completeness, scale, and recurring free-allowance checks.
      The sample demonstrates the interface; local import lets you inspect your
      own supplied files. Neither is proof that automatic checking works.
    </p><a class="text-link" href="/instagram-follow-back-checker/"
      >See what is available today <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a
    >
  </section>
  <section class="section-block guides-section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">A LITTLE CONTEXT GOES A LONG WAY</p><h2>
          Read before you interpret.
        </h2>
      </div><span class="muted">Practical guides, without the guesswork.</span>
    </div><div class="guide-grid">
      <a class="guide-card" href="/guides/followers-vs-following/"
        ><span>THE BASICS</span><h3>Followers vs. following</h3><p>
          Two directions, three useful groups. Start with a simple example.
        </p><span class="guide-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
      ><a class="guide-card" href="/guides/not-following-back-vs-unfollowed/"
        ><span>READING RESULTS</span><h3>
          Not following back isn’t the same as unfollowed
        </h3><p>
          What one report can tell you, and what requires a history.
        </p><span class="guide-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
      ><a class="guide-card" href="/guides/instagram-export-troubleshooting/"
        ><span>LOCAL IMPORTS</span><h3>When an export looks incomplete</h3><p>
          Missing directions, split files, and source dates explained.
        </p><span class="guide-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
      >
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>
